---
name: conformance-char-literal-escapes
---

# Escaped `Char` literals must survive every backend

A `Char` literal carrying an escape (`'\n'`, `'\t'`, `'\r'`, `'\\'`, `'"'`) has to reach
the generated code as an ESCAPE, not as the raw control character. On the JS lane it
did not: `JsGen`'s `Lit.Char` arm escaped only the double quote, so `'\n'` was written
into a JS string literal as a real newline and the emitted program failed to PARSE —
`SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected token`, before any of it ran.

That made every consumer of `std/parsing/layout` unusable on js, because
`layout.ssc:67` computes a column with `before.lastIndexOf('\n')`. It stayed invisible
while `dsl-yaml-like` was a corpus SKIP. (`jsgen-char-literal-escape`.)

The `Char`-as-argument positions below are the ones that broke; the `String` literals
alongside them are the control — `Lit.String` always carried the full escape chain, and
this case fails if either arm regresses.

```scalascript
def main() =
  // Char literal as an argument — the position that produced invalid JS.
  println("a\nb".lastIndexOf('\n'))
  println("a\tb".indexOf('\t'))
  println("a\\b".indexOf('\\'))
  println("a\"b".indexOf('"'))
  println("a\rb".indexOf('\r'))

  // Char literal as a value, and compared.
  val nl = '\n'
  println(nl == '\n')

  // Deliberately NOT `println('x')`: v2 renders a bare Char literal as its code point (`120`),
  // because on that lane a Char literal IS an Int — measured in `specs/v2-char-is-an-int.md`. That
  // is a separate defect with a separate fix, and folding it in here would leave this case red for a
  // reason that has nothing to do with the escaping it exists to gate.

  // String literals with the same escapes — the control side.
  println("x\ty".length)
  println("x\ny".length)
  println("q\\r".length)
```
